---
/**
 * The R32 handling fact, with its official sources.
 *
 * The brief: check with the partner before calling anyone certified, and link
 * the official source. The partner hasn't confirmed it (user, 5 Oct 2026), so
 * this states what NEA says and how the reader can ask. It makes no claim
 * about any firm. Sources checked on the date in company.json.
 */
import { company } from '../lib/data';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
---

<div class="flex gap-4 rounded-card border border-line bg-surface p-6">
  <span class="flex size-11 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-brand-light text-brand"><Icon name="shield" /></span>
  <div>
    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">R32 aircons and refrigerant handling</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      Many newer household aircons use R32, a refrigerant that is mildly flammable. The
      National Environment Agency (NEA) says that, to comply with SCDF’s requirements for
      handling R32 systems, service personnel should have completed a training course run
      by ITE: the
      <a href={company.r32.iteUrl} class="text-brand underline underline-offset-2">Certificate of Competency in Residential Air-Conditioning System (Refrigerant)</a>.
      NEA publishes a list of technicians who have completed it on
      <a href={company.r32.neaUrl} class="text-brand underline underline-offset-2">its refrigerants page</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      The refrigerant is named on the label of the outdoor unit. If yours uses R32, you can
      ask the firm whether the technician coming has done the course before work starts.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
